WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.872
Hello.

2
00:00:01.872 --> 00:00:06.096
A charging capacitor, the amount of salt in a tank,

3
00:00:06.096 --> 00:00:12.072
requests arriving at a server — all are described by a first-order linear equation.

4
00:00:12.072 --> 00:00:17.472
Today we learn a universal four-step method for it: the integrating factor.

5
00:00:17.472 --> 00:00:22.118
You will also need this algorithm for independent task two.

6
00:00:22.368 --> 00:00:28.920
Write the equation in normal form: y prime plus p of x times y equals q of x.

7
00:00:28.920 --> 00:00:32.280
Take mu equal to e to the integral of p.

8
00:00:32.280 --> 00:00:36.408
Its key property is that mu prime equals p times mu.

9
00:00:36.408 --> 00:00:43.008
So after multiplying by mu, the left side becomes the derivative of the product mu times y.

10
00:00:43.008 --> 00:00:46.166
The rest is plain integration.

11
00:00:46.416 --> 00:00:51.120
Example: y prime plus two y equals e to the x.

12
00:00:51.120 --> 00:00:57.120
Here p is two, its integral is two x, so mu equals e to the two x.

13
00:00:57.120 --> 00:00:59.976
Multiply the equation by mu.

14
00:00:59.976 --> 00:01:04.416
The left side is the derivative of e to the two x times y,

15
00:01:04.416 --> 00:01:10.766
and the right side is e to the two x times e to the x, that is e to the three x.

16
00:01:11.016 --> 00:01:17.928
Integrate both sides: e to the two x times y equals one third of e to the three x plus C.

17
00:01:17.928 --> 00:01:22.320
Careful: we add C right here, before dividing.

18
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.392
Then divide by e to the two x.

19
00:01:25.392 --> 00:01:31.286
y equals one third of e to the x plus C times e to the minus two x.

20
00:01:31.536 --> 00:01:34.368
Notice the structure of the answer.

21
00:01:34.368 --> 00:01:39.408
The first term is a particular solution of the nonhomogeneous equation.

22
00:01:39.408 --> 00:01:43.632
The second is the general solution of the homogeneous one.

23
00:01:43.632 --> 00:01:50.088
Check: the derivative is one third of e to the x minus two C e to the minus two x.

24
00:01:50.088 --> 00:01:55.358
Adding two y, the C terms cancel and e to the x remains.

25
00:01:55.608 --> 00:01:58.848
The graph shows several integral curves.

26
00:01:58.848 --> 00:02:06.192
As x grows, they all approach one third e to the x because C e to the minus two x decays rapidly.

27
00:02:06.192 --> 00:02:14.184
Here C e to the minus two x is the transient part, while one third e to the x is the forced, or asymptotic,

28
00:02:14.184 --> 00:02:16.080
response.

29
00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:22.934
Since that response itself grows, it should not be called a constant steady state in the strict sense.

30
00:02:23.184 --> 00:02:28.416
Check question: find the integrating factor for the equation on the screen.

31
00:02:28.416 --> 00:02:32.582
First make sure the coefficient of y prime is one.
